<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1539">1539</a>–<a href="/years/1616">1616</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as El Inca Garcilaso</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Garcilaso de la Vega was a writer born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> to a Spanish conquistador and an Inca noblewoman of the royal family. Settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1560, he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-commentaries-of-the-incas">Comentarios Reales de los Incas</a>, published in 1609, a history of the Incas and the Spanish conquest. He is regarded as the first great writer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega.jpg" alt="Inca Garcilaso de la Vega" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlos Penoso Sc.[1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inca_Garcilaso_1879.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Gómez Suárez de Figueroa on 12 April 1539 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>. His father was the Spanish captain Sebastián Garcilaso de la Vega y Vargas and his mother the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ñusta Palla Chimpu Ocllo, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>. Because his parents were not married in the Church, he was considered illegitimate and was given only his mother's surname. His first language was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>. After ten years with his mother's family his father took him into his household and gave him an education under Juan de Cuéllar, a canon of the Cathedral of Cusco. In 1554 he became secretary to his father, then corregidor of Cusco. After his father's death in 1559 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> at the age of 21.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> he went to Montilla, where his father's brother Alonso de Vargas became his protector, and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> to seek acknowledgement from the Crown as his father's son. He was allowed to take the name Garcilaso de la Vega, which helped him enter Spanish society, and he was known as El Inca. His education was informal, and he never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In 1570 he entered Spanish military service, fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpujarras">Alpujarras</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> after the Morisco Revolt, and received the rank of captain. He had at least two sons by servants; one, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a>, born in 1590, helped him copy the Royal Commentaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His first work, La Florida del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>, was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1605. It describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a>'s expedition in Florida and defends both Spanish sovereignty and the dignity and rationality of Native Americans. Historians such as Jerald T. Milanich and Charles M. Hudson note serious problems with its sequence of events and the locations of towns.</p><p>His best-known work, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-commentaries-of-the-incas">Comentarios Reales de los Incas</a>, was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1609, with a second part on the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> published in 1617. It rests mostly on what his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> relatives told him in childhood and partly on the history of Blas Valera. He portrays the Incas as benevolent rulers of a well-fed country, describes their system of tribute and labour, and does not discuss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>. In the 1780s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> banned its publication in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, and the book was not reprinted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> until 1918. It was first published in English in 1685. Nineteenth-century Peruvian scholars treated it more sceptically. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, recorded as 23 April 1616.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inca Garcilaso de la Vega” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Accounts by Pedro Cieza de León, Juan de Betanzos, Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala and <b>Garcilaso de la Vega</b>, whose Comentarios Reales de los Incas appeared in 1609, made the Incas known in Europe, and Inti Raymi is still celebrated at Cusco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial Peru also produced classic accounts of the Andean past, the Royal Commentaries of the Incas by <b>Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</b>, published in 1609, and the illustrated chronicle of Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, while the Cuzco School of painting blended European and Andean styles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>One legend collected by the chronicler <b>Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</b> has Manco Capac and Mama Ocllo sent by the Sun God, emerging from the lake.</blockquote><a href="/religion/inca-religion">Inca religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1589" aria-label="Cusco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Inca Garcilaso de la Vega"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4011 6145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4011 6145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1589">What was here in 1589? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
